<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>This fictional modern episode addresses the presenting concerns below. [S]</paragraph><list><item>He wants to stay steady on his feet and keep making public appearances. [S]</item><item>People in crowds have trouble hearing him because his voice is soft. [S]</item></list><paragraph>Historical basis. These claims are historical evidence, not findings from a modern examination.</paragraph><list><item>Muhammad Ali was born Cassius Clay in Louisville, Kentucky, on January 17, 1942. [D]</item><item>In 1984, at age 42 and three years after he retired from boxing, Ali was diagnosed with a parkinsonian condition by the neurologist Stanley Fahn in New York; it has been publicly described as Parkinson&apos;s disease since. [D]</item><item>The diagnosing neurologist later said publicly that slowness of movement was Ali&apos;s main limitation at the time of diagnosis, with a masked face, decreased blinking, a typical parkinsonian tremor, and a shuffling gait, and that some features were too early for classic Parkinson&apos;s disease. [D]</item><item>In a 1996 television interview, Ali&apos;s physician Dennis Cope described his condition as Parkinson&apos;s syndrome attributed to pugilistic brain syndrome from boxing, and said testing showed his cognitive function had not declined: &quot;His mind is fine.&quot; [D]</item><item>Whether boxing caused Ali&apos;s Parkinson&apos;s disease is disputed. The diagnosing neurologist thought the early symptoms were probably caused by repeated blows to the head but could not be certain; his later physician Abraham Lieberman did not think boxing contributed, though he was not a hundred percent certain; and the director of the Muhammad Ali Parkinson Center said there was no medical evidence that boxing contributed. [R]</item></list><paragraph>3 later historical claims are omitted because they describe events after the index age. See the teaching note.</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.20" extension="2015-08-01"/>
    <code code="11348-0" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Past illness note"/>
    <title>Past Medical History</title>
    <text><paragraph>Historical biography with true dates. These events are not part of the synthetic care timeline.</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Historical date</th><th>History</th><th>Evidence</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1981</td><td>Professional boxing career with repeated blows to the head, ended by retirement in 1981; whether it caused his Parkinson&apos;s disease is disputed</td><td>R (CLM-0003, CLM-0006)</td></tr><tr><td>1984</td><td>Parkinsonian condition diagnosed in 1984, at 42, by a neurologist in New York, with slowness of movement, a masked face, decreased blinking, tremor, and a shuffling gait</td><td>D (CLM-0003, CLM-0004)</td></tr></tbody></table></text>

  </section>
</component>

